{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01MTLPJVPBj5N3nauSSLPVZB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`ai-engineering/answers/01-006-what-are-scaling-laws-and-why-do-they-matter.md`** — Q6: What are scaling laws and why do they matter?\n\n**Key content in the note:**\n- **Concept:** Power-law relationships between compute (C), model size (N), training tokens (D), and loss — predictable and empirically verified\n- **Kaplan 2020 (OpenAI):** Original finding — bias toward larger models, train less. Drove the GPT-3 era\n- **Chinchilla 2022 (DeepMind):** Corrected Kaplan — N and D must scale proportionally (~20 tokens/param). 70B Chinchilla beat 280B Gopher on 4× fewer params with 4× more training\n- **Practical formula:** optimal N ≈ √(C/6), D ≈ 20N for a fixed compute budget C\n- **Real examples:** LLaMA 2 (70B, 2T tokens) as Chinchilla-optimal; Mistral outperforming larger undertrained models\n- **Limits:** Data wall (~3–5T filtered tokens), irreducible Bayes-optimal loss floor\n- **3 pitfalls flagged:** citing only Kaplan, no production connection, missing the data wall\n\nNext up in the queue: **Q7 — What is temperature and top-p sampling? How do they affect outputs?**"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":253,"cache_read_input_tokens":55084,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19d87fca-0057-4a2e-b17c-03ac7cb99321","uuid":"c3e8b12b-bb5c-49d8-b20f-e8ead24b9941"}
